# Social sign-in

`howdy_auth` can sign people in with an account they already have
elsewhere. Five providers are built in:

- Google
- Sign in with Apple
- GitHub
- Facebook
- Microsoft Entra ID

A provider sign-in makes an ordinary Howdy session. The same users,
[groups](/docs/auth/groups), suspension rules and
[guard](/docs/auth/overview) apply, and Howdy never stores the provider's
access or refresh tokens. For a customer's own identity provider, such as
Okta, see [Enterprise single sign-on](/docs/auth/sso) instead.

## Adding Google

Register an OAuth *Web application* client in Google Cloud, and configure
its consent screen. Then add the provider as the app starts, and mount the
provider routes:

```gleam
import howdy/auth
import howdy/auth/pages
import howdy/auth/providers/google
import howdy/auth/routes

pub fn app(identity) {
  let assert Ok(identity) =
    auth.with_provider(
      identity,
      google.new(client_id: google_client_id, client_secret: google_secret),
    )

  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.controller(routes.api(identity, at: "/api/auth"))
  |> howdy.controller(routes.providers(
    identity,
    at: "/auth",
    success_path: "/auth/account",
    failure_path: "/auth/login",
  ))
  |> howdy.controller(pages.routes(identity, at: "/auth", api_at: "/api/auth"))
}
```

Load the client id and secret from your secret configuration, never from
source control. Register this exact callback URL with Google:

```
https://app.example.com/auth/providers/google/callback
```

It is your configured origin, the mount path, and
`/providers/<id>/callback`. In development,
`http://localhost:8787/auth/providers/google/callback` works too. Google
needs Erlang/OTP 27 or newer, and asks only for `openid email`.

Mount the provider routes at the same path as the
[starter pages](/docs/auth/overview), and their sign-in buttons and
**Link** forms appear by themselves. For pages of your own,
`auth.providers(identity)` lists each provider's id and display name.

To only let in one Google Workspace, check the signed `hd` claim:

```gleam
google.new(client_id:, client_secret:)
|> google.require_hosted_domain("example.com")
```

## The other providers

They are added the same way, with `auth.with_provider`:

```gleam
import howdy/auth/providers/entra
import howdy/auth/providers/facebook
import howdy/auth/providers/github

github.new(client_id: github_id, client_secret: github_secret)
facebook.new(client_id: facebook_app_id, client_secret: facebook_secret)
entra.new(
  client_id: entra_id,
  client_secret: entra_secret,
  tenant: "organizations",
)
```

Their callbacks follow the same pattern:
`/auth/providers/github/callback`, `/auth/providers/facebook/callback` and
`/auth/providers/entra/callback`.

Entra's `tenant` is a directory id, a domain such as
`example.onmicrosoft.com`, or one of `common`, `organizations` and
`consumers`. The app registration must allow the account types that tenant
selects.

### Sign in with Apple

```gleam
import howdy/auth/providers/apple

let assert Ok(identity) =
  auth.with_provider(
    identity,
    apple.new(
      client_id: "com.example.web",
      team_id: "ABCDE12345",
      key_id: "KEY1234567",
      private_key: apple_p8_contents,
    ),
  )
```

- `client_id` is the *Services ID*, not an app's bundle id.
- `private_key` is the text of the `.p8` key you download from Apple. Keep
  it with your other secrets. Howdy signs a short-lived token with it for
  each sign-in, and signs one as the app starts, so a key that does not
  work is an error there.
- Apple requires HTTPS and rejects `localhost`, so test on a real or
  tunnelled domain.

Apple sends its answer back as a cross-site form `POST`, on which browsers
leave out `SameSite=Lax` cookies. The callback route turns that `POST`
into a redirect to itself as a `GET`, and decides nothing until then. If
you put [CSRF middleware](/docs/middleware/csrf) of your own in front of
the auth routes, exempt `POST /auth/providers/apple/callback`.

Apple may hand out a private relay address that forwards to the user.
Mail to a relay address only arrives from sender domains you have
registered with Apple, so register yours, or sign-in emails will not
arrive.

## Which accounts a provider can reach

Providers are trusted to say who somebody is, not always to say which
mailbox they own. So:

- A provider identity that is already linked signs in to its account, even
  if its email address has since changed. It never changes the account's
  own address.
- **A new identity** creates an account only when
  [registration](/docs/auth/overview) is on and the provider has verified
  the address. Google counts Gmail and verified Workspace addresses; GitHub
  and Apple count the addresses they report as verified. Facebook and Entra
  never create accounts.
- **An address that already has an account is never linked
  automatically.** Its owner signs in the usual way first, then links the
  provider from the account page.

Each account can link one identity per provider. Linking an identity that
belongs to another account fails; it never replaces a link.

## Linking and unlinking

On the starter `/auth/account` page, each provider has a **Link** button.
It posts to `/auth/providers/<id>/link`, which only works from a session
made within the last ten minutes (`fresh_session_seconds` in the
[policy](/docs/auth/sessions)), and the same session must finish the link.

`GET /providers` on the JSON API lists the linked providers, and
`POST /providers/unlink` with `{"issuer":"…"}` removes one. Unlinking needs a
recent sign-in through a *different* method, so a stolen provider session
cannot remove the other ways in. See
[Account changes](/docs/auth/account).

## Without email at all

For an app that only signs in with providers, create auth without a
`deliver` function:

```gleam
let assert Ok(identity) = auth.new_without_email(repo: db, origin: origin)
```

Email tokens are then off, and the starter pages hide their email forms.
`auth.with_email_tokens(identity, deliver: send_email)` turns them on later.

## Rules the routes follow

- The routes that start a sign-in are `POST`s and require your `origin`;
  plain HTML forms work.
- Each attempt uses a one-time state, a nonce, PKCE where the provider
  supports it, and its own `HttpOnly` cookie. It expires after ten minutes,
  and is spent before the provider is contacted.
- `success_path` and `failure_path` are fixed paths in your app, never taken
  from the request. A failure redirects without saying why.
- The routes allow 30 requests a minute per client. Behind a proxy, use
  `routes.providers_limited_by` with the header your proxy sets, as for
  [the JSON API](/docs/auth/administration).
- The callback's query string holds a one-time code. Keep it out of your
  own request logs.

Signing out of your app does not sign the browser out of the provider.

A session made through a provider is recorded with the method
`provider:<id>`, such as `provider:google`. It does not count as the fresh
email sign-in needed to [set a password](/docs/auth/passwords).

## Without HTTP

`auth.begin_provider`, `auth.begin_provider_link` and `auth.finish_provider`
run the same steps for a transport of your own. `finish_provider` returns
`ProviderSession(session)`, `ProviderLinked`, or `ProviderSecondFactor(…)`
when the user must also pass [a second factor](/docs/auth/mfa). Your
transport is then responsible for the browser cookie, `origin` checks and
rate limits.
